00:03:37.749 --> 00:03:38.640
3:37
Jacob: Right. Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:46.000
so these are a couple of the levels. Let’s&nbsp;
back up to how you even got to understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.840
these things. Did you have a history? I know&nbsp;
this, but my listeners may not. Can we talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.640
a little bit about your history of understanding&nbsp;
your own body and diving more into these things?&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:58.960
3:57
Cristine: Yeah. So I had a chemical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:08.000
imbalance growing up, and that showed up for me&nbsp;
as chronic depression — almost incapacitating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:15.920
anxiety. I barely got to school in junior high.&nbsp;
I had so much anxiety, peer pressure, things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:24.800
were just beyond… I didn’t even talk about them&nbsp;
because I was so blocked in my ability to process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:32.080
and get through that. And so I actually turned&nbsp;
to substances by the time I was in high school.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:35.920
I just basically threw my hands in the air like,&nbsp;
“I just don’t think I’m ever going to get better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:48.240
using what I know.” And so I went to unknowns&nbsp;
and even no-nos to try to self-medicate. I found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:57.600
that it did change my brain, and I went on this&nbsp;
quest to find the substance that had the impact I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:07.200
was looking for. And for me, that became my first&nbsp;
career: substance abuse counseling. And I can tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:11.440
you how often people are self-medicating without&nbsp;
even realizing they’re self-medicating. They just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:19.600
want to feel better. They want energy if they’re&nbsp;
tired. They want to feel happy if they live in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:28.160
this dense, heavy depression. They’re looking&nbsp;
to function if anxiety is getting in the way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:38.160
basic life. So that’s typically why people get&nbsp;
into something that’s physically changing them.&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:44.240
That was my first experience with understanding&nbsp;
the physical. And so I wrote the book 10 Ways to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:51.760
Balance Your Brain Chemistry after my whole&nbsp;
life was transformed and I realized that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:57.440
chemical imbalance didn’t all go away, even&nbsp;
though the substances weren’t the solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:06.960
anymore. I still had chronic depression. I still&nbsp;
had bouts of anxiety. So I discovered natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:14.000
ways — higher frequency ways — to resolve those&nbsp;
chemical imbalances. That’s what I talk about in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:21.520
the physical body. You can alter your physical&nbsp;
wellbeing through a lot of different things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:29.200
and if you go a natural route, you’re more likely&nbsp;
to have better, longer-term solutions without side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:33.600
effects or other consequences.
6:36&nbsp;

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:42.320
Jacob: What did you discover in your long path&nbsp;
of dealing with substance abuse and helping other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:59.280
people, and with your own journey? Now that it’s&nbsp;
been so long between your years as a substance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:04.360
abuse counselor and the things you were doing…&nbsp;
what would you say that you learned in big part?&nbsp;

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:05.040
7:05
Cristine:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:10.960
Well, that’s where I started. If you break down&nbsp;
the chemistry — and let’s talk about endorphins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:19.520
because your show is about love — it’s tricky to&nbsp;
look at how to correct endorphins. Serotonin is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:26.507
one that we talk about a lot more. Serotonin&nbsp;
is a physical condition, a chemical that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:40.800
gives you a sense of wellbeing, and you can get&nbsp;
it from your food, your diet — carbs, comfort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:51.920
foods. There’s a book called Potatoes Not Prozac&nbsp;
that says a lot in the title. That was one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.920 --> 00:08:02.240
first primers that was a launchpad for 10 Ways. I&nbsp;
was figuring out that what I was eating mattered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:06.960
and the carbs I was going to were actually&nbsp;
causing my depression because I had a wheat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:17.120
sensitivity. This was in the nineties. We weren’t&nbsp;
talking about gluten-free then, but it made a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:25.200
huge difference for me to change my diet. But how&nbsp;
would I know that? That had to be informed through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:32.160
a higher source. I was also on a spiritual&nbsp;
journey at the time. What I found is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:43.360
I can actually be led to solutions in my physical&nbsp;
wellbeing through a higher source — a truth that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:52.640
if I’m aware and open to it, can come through.
That’s the serotonin side. What I figured out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:57.600
also is that it wasn’t really the serotonin&nbsp;
that was out of balance for me — it was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:07.440
endorphins. Potatoes Not Prozac talks about that&nbsp;
too. Serotonin is influenced by carbs and comfort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:14.960
foods, but the endorphins… I’m going to see if&nbsp;
you can guess. It stands for “inner orphan,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:24.960
which is like morphine, right? Endorphins. This&nbsp;
is your pain receptors and feel-good chemistry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:33.120
but highly addictive, like narcotics. Do&nbsp;
you know the food group that fits with this?&nbsp;

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:35.906
9:34
Jacob: Oh, like alcohol?&nbsp;

00:09:35.906 --> 00:09:37.600
9:35
Cristine: Close. Alcohol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:50.560
affects GABA levels. Sugar. Sugar is actually on&nbsp;
the continuum of narcotics. It’s a narcotic. Isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:56.800
that interesting? That made so much sense for me&nbsp;
to understand that I was addicted to sugar as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:09.680
kid. So many people try to get off of it and go&nbsp;
through serious withdrawals. That’s been a battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:20.560
for me — to be the master of my endorphin pathways&nbsp;
and not let sugar take over or the cravings.&nbsp;

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:26.640
10:24
Jacob: So you went through this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:32.320
journey of discovering what different substances&nbsp;
even are. It wasn’t just alcohol or drugs. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:40.960
discovered addictions to food… Did it expand into&nbsp;
understanding addictions to patterns or even our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:42.720
relationships with one another?
10:45&nbsp;

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:51.360
Cristine: Yeah, there are two areas I think are&nbsp;
worth looking at. One is dopamine. Dopamine is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:57.360
very different chemical than endorphins, and&nbsp;
they’re both highly addictive. Dopamine is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:09.360
more charged. My drug of choice was cocaine, so&nbsp;
that fits in the dopamine category. It numbs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:18.400
creates that high, but you can’t sustain dopamine.&nbsp;
You can’t be high at that level all the time — it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:32.800
will actually turn into psychosis if you try.&nbsp;
Those big bursts are what some of us go after.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:47.600
Gambling is one of those. It helped me understand&nbsp;
I was bipolar. I got that diagnosis at one point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:54.640
and I had to become the master of my dopamine&nbsp;
as well, because I realized if I come up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.640 --> 00:12:01.600
I will go down. The trick wasn’t to try&nbsp;
to change it and stay up. The trick was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:09.680
realize when I was down, there was some work to&nbsp;
do, some healing to do, and not just try to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:18.880
back up — but allow myself to find that natural&nbsp;
middle path in living. That was a big lesson.&nbsp;

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:30.240
The other one is with endorphins — that’s where&nbsp;
the love addict comes in. Sugar is the chemical or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:39.920
physical fix with endorphins, but on the emotional&nbsp;
level it’s typically we’re seeking love. For a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.920 --> 00:12:53.760
lot of us that’s romantic love, or some kind of&nbsp;
belonging or security — that warm, fuzzy thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.400 --> 00:13:05.760
And it’s not necessarily wrong. One of the things&nbsp;
I found is what I call the spiral. The spiral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:11.600
includes things you want to do abstinence with&nbsp;
on the outside — heroin is probably not a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:25.360
idea on the endorphin continuum. You’re going to&nbsp;
let that go. The next layer inward is moderation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:39.680
I had to find a way to do moderation with this&nbsp;
romantic craving. You get into the honeymoon and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:50.080
you want to stay there, and then it fluctuates.&nbsp;
Some of that is moving past the addictive nature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:56.800
of love and into the third part of the cycle,&nbsp;
which is attainment. Attainment is doing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:05.840
work to have longer-term real solutions.&nbsp;
In a relationship it’s not just “we love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:12.560
the honeymoon and then it gets hard — do we&nbsp;
stay or do we go?” With the attainment part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:21.040
it closes that gap, that inner hole which&nbsp;
is really what we’re trying to fill, to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:31.200
solution that’s more solid — a deeper love, a more&nbsp;
complete sense of self. It could be self-love.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:37.760
You start getting into that center with that.
14:37&nbsp;

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:45.760
Jacob: With these cycles, what are we trying to&nbsp;
complete with these cycles ideally? What is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:47.620
that we’re trying to get?
14:47&nbsp;

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:58.960
Cristine: It is a healing — an inner healing. I&nbsp;
actually developed this spiral model when I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:07.920
working in a jail. I took my recovery and started&nbsp;
creating a curriculum with these concepts as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:15.360
substance abuse counselor. I was teaching this,&nbsp;
creating this, as I was working with inmates. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:26.240
of them told me they had even done this attainment&nbsp;
thing. They’d say, “We did twelve years sober&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:34.480
doing the AA thing,” and some of the attainment&nbsp;
things are self-help programs or religious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:41.200
commitments or health and wellness commitments —&nbsp;
things you’re probably not going to get addicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:55.120
to, like going to the gym, or education.&nbsp;
They were doing all these things right and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.120 --> 00:16:04.240
they still relapsed. I took it as: this is the&nbsp;
challenge. I had twenty years clean by the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:10.320
I was teaching this, so I felt pretty stable, but&nbsp;
there were areas of my life, like relationships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:16.000
that I hadn’t figured out. I kept getting into&nbsp;
unhealthy relationships. I knew that if I were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:27.040
to figure this out, that was the challenge.
I’d gone through the attainment model myself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:33.360
and I remember there was a night I left the&nbsp;
jail and my boyfriend broke up with me as I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:45.040
was driving home. It was a Friday night, going&nbsp;
home alone. What’s interesting is I was on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:53.280
spiritual journey — I was studying Kabbalah — and&nbsp;
it happened to be the moment of Yom Kippur. Yom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.280 --> 00:17:04.480
Kippur in the Jewish tradition starts at sundown&nbsp;
and is a 24-hour Day of Atonement. If you look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:14.960
that word, atonement is “at-one-ment.” It’s a&nbsp;
devotion after Rosh Hashanah. You’ve done ten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:30.880
days of deep diving, excavating your past year&nbsp;
and where you could improve. This 24 hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:38.320
people typically dress in white, they fast,&nbsp;
they don’t work or engage in regular life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:46.720
stuff — they just spend that 24 hours in divine&nbsp;
connection. I got home and realized, “Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:54.160
well I guess I don’t know what this means, but&nbsp;
I choose this.” I was lying on my couch and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.160 --> 00:18:04.400
sun went down. I remember there was this light —&nbsp;
it almost entered like a shaft through the top of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:16.800
my head and poured through. It felt like my spine,&nbsp;
from head to toe. I felt light enter me. And that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:26.400
was the solution. The solution of this spiral&nbsp;
is at this center: really, all that matters is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:36.320
that you are whole and complete through a divine&nbsp;
connection, and you bring your whole self to it.&nbsp;

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:44.080
18:41
Jacob: Can I ask — I’m not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:49.120
sure what language you typically use for this&nbsp;
now — do you call this a Kundalini awakening?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:55.105
Obviously spiritual awakening is terminology&nbsp;
you’ve used, but have you dove into Kundalini?&nbsp;

00:18:55.105 --> 00:18:56.240
18:55
Cristine: I have. I love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.240 --> 00:19:11.840
Kundalini. Kundalini was very supportive. There&nbsp;
was a phase in my life where my healing journey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:21.680
was very much influenced by it. My experience with&nbsp;
that, though, is that the Kundalini was more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.680 --> 00:19:28.080
making corrections in my vital body. The vital&nbsp;
body contains the chakras, meridians, and your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:39.040
aura field. That’s a layer of us that is very much&nbsp;
neglected by Western medicine, but important in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.040 --> 00:19:48.000
influencing the physical self and connected to&nbsp;
the quantum self. So that’s where I would place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.000 --> 00:20:01.840
Kundalini — a very effective vital body tool and&nbsp;
process to clear and strengthen the vital body.&nbsp;

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:06.320
20:03
Jacob: When you had your first kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.320 --> 00:20:15.520
of initial awakening… have you always had some&nbsp;
sort of spiritual side to you? Did you grow up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:20.545
in a religion? Were you always spiritual, or&nbsp;
was this kind of a later-in-life awakening?&nbsp;

00:20:20.545 --> 00:20:22.800
20:20
Cristine: That’s a great question. I grew up in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.800 --> 00:20:34.000
religion, Christian-based, and I followed as much&nbsp;
as I could with it. But like I just shared, my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:43.920
chemistry was not working, and religion didn’t fix&nbsp;
it. So I left religion in my path with substance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.920 --> 00:20:51.760
use. Here’s what’s kind of cool — and I’m going&nbsp;
to release my recovery story, so I don’t want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:59.680
give it all away because that’s going to come out.&nbsp;
It’s called Yearning for Freedom. In my late teens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:06.880
and early young adult life, I just wanted freedom&nbsp;
from the guilt, because that is one of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:14.480
that happens with someone who’s got a religious&nbsp;
influence and is fighting against it or steering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.480 --> 00:21:21.520
away from it. I wanted freedom from guilt, but&nbsp;
I also wanted freedom from pain and suffering.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:26.640
I just thought if I could just find this, it’s got&nbsp;
to be out there. I was looking definitely outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:34.960
myself, but not only outside myself — down,&nbsp;
frequency-wise. I wasn’t looking up. And so I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.960 --> 00:21:45.120
found cocaine. It was a great solution for a few&nbsp;
months, and then it started taking a toll on me.&nbsp;

00:21:46.960 --> 00:21:50.080
When you talk about spiritual things, I think&nbsp;
it’s important to look at the dark as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.080 --> 00:22:00.160
the light. There was a moment when I had used this&nbsp;
drug for about eight months or so and still could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:08.880
kind of take it or leave it — I could still do&nbsp;
life and then wait for the weekend. But there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.880 --> 00:22:16.960
was a night I remember very specifically. I&nbsp;
was with a couple people and we were using,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:16.960 --> 00:22:24.240
and a friend came to the door. This friend&nbsp;
had been bubbly, energetic. She didn’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:31.920
depression like I dealt with… until we opened&nbsp;
the door that night and her whole demeanor was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:39.280
different. Her vibration was different. Even&nbsp;
then I could sense, “What happened?” She’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:48.880
come back from a funeral — a friend of hers who&nbsp;
had overdosed — and she just couldn’t shake it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.160 --> 00:23:00.960
She came over and joined the party, and I remember&nbsp;
that almost in an instant I’d gone from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:12.400
freedom I had with this drug to being controlled&nbsp;
by it. Theoretically — and this is just me looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:22.320
back — I believe the person who overdosed came&nbsp;
with her and walked through the door. So this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:34.320
entity, this earthbound essence, attached to&nbsp;
me. After that point in time I couldn’t say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.320 --> 00:23:41.840
no. I didn’t have power over what was going on. I&nbsp;
started losing weight. I started experiencing all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:41.840 --> 00:23:52.960
the late-stage consequences of my use. That’s my&nbsp;
theory. People may or may not get that. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:52.960 --> 00:24:01.360
actually a book that coincides with this called&nbsp;
Return from Tomorrow. Richie is the name of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:18.160
author. He talks about his near-death experience&nbsp;
where he was moving in and out. He stepped into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:18.160 --> 00:24:27.200
bar and watched people sitting around the bar, but&nbsp;
there were also earthbounds sitting around the bar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:27.200 --> 00:24:33.680
with them, waiting for them to drink to a certain&nbsp;
point, and then it was like they kind of unzipped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:40.868
them and entered to vicariously continue to&nbsp;
have that experience. Isn’t that interesting?&nbsp;

00:24:40.868 --> 00:24:41.440
24:40
Jacob: That’s a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.440 --> 00:24:46.080
interesting way to look at it. I feel like it&nbsp;
coincides with my experience as well — back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:59.760
in my days of drinking and partying and nights of&nbsp;
cocaine. So what do you think that is? Do you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.760 --> 00:25:08.640
that as a positive way of addressing these things&nbsp;
that people are dealing with? How can you spot it?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:08.640 --> 00:25:15.680
If someone comes to you nowadays and maybe they&nbsp;
have one of these entities hanging over them…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:21.480
what’s your approach if you deem that’s the&nbsp;
case? How can we shake off these entities?&nbsp;

00:25:21.480 --> 00:25:23.040
25:22
Cristine: This is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:30.240
where we start talking quantum things. In Quantum&nbsp;
University they refer to this as perverse energy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:40.400
You’re working at the spiritual level. Sometimes&nbsp;
it’s generational. I’ve had clients who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:41.040 --> 00:25:47.200
affected by something that’s always been there. As&nbsp;
we tune into that — and I use a lot of intuition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:57.680
with this — sometimes I’ll just know it’s an&nbsp;
ancestor, like a great-grandfather or grandmother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.240 --> 00:26:03.200
I’ll say certain things and they’ll be like,&nbsp;
“Oh yeah, I’ve heard that — my mom’s mom had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:13.600
depression,” or whatever. Sometimes I’m actually&nbsp;
doing therapy on both. We invite them to stay and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:19.200
experience the healing. That’s quantum stuff.
Kundalini is really interesting when you say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.200 --> 00:26:27.840
that. My first Kundalini session was actually&nbsp;
geared toward addiction release. The series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:37.760
was done by a gentleman who was using it for&nbsp;
addiction recovery as well. I remember starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:37.760 --> 00:26:51.600
the process and experiencing my grandfather. What&nbsp;
I did in that Kundalini session actually cleared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:51.600 --> 00:27:01.920
something for him. He had passed away with a lot&nbsp;
of heavy emotion — we knew he wasn’t well when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.920 --> 00:27:10.480
left. It cleared it. It was amazing. So that’s&nbsp;
