00:29:32.960 --> 00:29:41.280
the four bodies — twice as effective&nbsp;
as EMDR. So yeah, it’s good stuff.&nbsp;

00:29:41.280 --> 00:29:45.200
29:41
Jacob: That’s awesome. I want to add just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:45.200 --> 00:29:51.040
one more time back on this entities thing. I think&nbsp;
it’s always a very interesting thing to explore:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.920 --> 00:29:56.800
what thoughts aren’t my own, what are old&nbsp;
patterns or ways of thinking that don’t serve me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.800 --> 00:30:03.040
what is my intuition versus maybe a negative&nbsp;
thought pattern or a negative entity or something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:12.000
that’s not me. I don’t necessarily have to get in&nbsp;
the ring with boxing gloves, but I can treat it as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.000 --> 00:30:17.440
separate from me. Can you help explore for anybody&nbsp;
listening — how do we delineate these things? How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.440 --> 00:30:20.160
do we separate what’s us and our intuition?
30:20&nbsp;

00:30:20.160 --> 00:30:26.320
Cristine: Perfect. We’re stepping right into the&nbsp;
mental body. This is so interesting, and I hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:26.320 --> 00:30:33.200
people grab the Four Bodies, Four Medicines ebook&nbsp;
to really explore this too. The example I use in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:33.200 --> 00:30:45.360
there is: if we try to use the mind to correct&nbsp;
the mind, we can get just a lot of chaos going on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:47.840 --> 00:30:55.440
It’s trying to go back and forth between positive&nbsp;
thinking and negative thinking. “Oh, there I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:55.440 --> 00:30:59.200
doing negative thinking,” and then the judge comes&nbsp;
in: “You shouldn’t be negative thinking.” So we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:59.200 --> 00:31:03.200
have that voice, and then “You better be positive&nbsp;
thinking,” and we have that voice. Just a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:03.200 --> 00:31:16.480
chaos. The solution I found for that is it goes&nbsp;
back to zero. It’s actually neutrality. Neutrality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:16.480 --> 00:31:24.720
has the option of not charging your thoughts&nbsp;
with anything. I like playing the neutral game.&nbsp;

00:31:28.560 --> 00:31:38.800
The neutral game is a way to bring the mind to the&nbsp;
present moment with zero emotion — just neutral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:38.800 --> 00:31:46.560
state, present moment. If I’m playing the game in&nbsp;
my setting right here, I’m going to say: “Okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:46.560 --> 00:31:55.600
there is a clock in front of me.” I can give the&nbsp;
time. I can look around and see there are four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:55.600 --> 00:32:10.320
frames on my wall. My chair is black. Then I can&nbsp;
go into auditory senses: I hear some white noise…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:13.440 --> 00:32:21.600
I might see something out the window.&nbsp;
You’re bringing yourself back to “Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:34.400
I can feel.” If I put my toes on the ground, I can&nbsp;
feel my chair protector. I can feel the texture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:34.400 --> 00:32:50.080
of my chair. You have to slow down to be able to&nbsp;
identify the experience, and you avoid qualifiers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:50.080 --> 00:33:03.040
or adjectives that add emotion to it. The goal is&nbsp;
just: there are four drawers. The water bottle is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:03.040 --> 00:33:18.800
blue. I smell… You can do it in your room. You&nbsp;
can do it while you’re driving. A lot of times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:19.600 --> 00:33:32.160
when I was driving and had a commute, I’d go into&nbsp;
some headspace. Just practicing this: sky is blue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:45.360
there’s a white car in front of me, turn off the&nbsp;
radio, and just be in the silence. The trick is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:45.360 --> 00:33:51.360
you’re actually changing your neural pathways&nbsp;
with it — like a mindfulness exercise, basically.&nbsp;

00:33:51.360 --> 00:33:54.800
33:51
Jacob: I think it helps people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:54.800 --> 00:34:01.520
really notice, because when you start to stack&nbsp;
on a few of these different sense techniques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:01.520 --> 00:34:09.120
you actually realize you’ve been smelling this&nbsp;
whole time but you didn’t really notice… You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.120 --> 00:34:16.560
have the taste of coffee or whatever in your&nbsp;
mouth… When you’re naming things, either saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:16.560 --> 00:34:24.480
them out loud or at least identifying them, what&nbsp;
happens is a lot of people suddenly get pulled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:30.640
out of their thought loops. They didn’t realize&nbsp;
they’d just been in this world of only thinking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:33.440 --> 00:34:43.760
Thinking is a sense, just like smelling and&nbsp;
seeing, but I think we give it so much power. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:43.760 --> 00:34:55.600
the ultimate always-thinking sense. We get stuck&nbsp;
in thinking and ignore the other senses. That’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:55.600 --> 00:35:02.785
where the thought loops and old patterns can take&nbsp;
place when we’re stuck in just the thinking mode.&nbsp;

00:35:02.785 --> 00:35:04.240
35:02
Cristine: Yeah.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:04.240 --> 00:35:13.600
Mindfulness is a way into the present moment. It&nbsp;
took me a while to get to meditation as a practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:13.600 --> 00:35:25.120
of stillness and silence. For me, my mind was so&nbsp;
busy. The first thing I got to do was yoga. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:25.120 --> 00:35:36.640
so divine — I knew it was God — because I had to&nbsp;
be with people for almost a year to get it. I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:44.160
supporting a woman in recovery. I would support&nbsp;
her with principles, and then she would tell me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:44.160 --> 00:35:50.400
“Oh, you should go do yoga,” and she would ask me&nbsp;
every week, “Have you done yoga?” By the time we’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:50.400 --> 00:35:55.680
worked together for several months I was like, “I&nbsp;
have to go do yoga so I can at least answer yes.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:55.680 --> 00:36:02.080
So I got the book by Baron Baptiste, Journey Into&nbsp;
Power, and that’s how I started. I didn’t go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:02.080 --> 00:36:11.120
a yoga studio — I just started at home. Reading&nbsp;
his book, I was compelled. I was like, “Oh, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:11.120 --> 00:36:19.040
is a solution.” He talked about the gap between&nbsp;
stimulus and response. Basically he’s talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:19.040 --> 00:36:26.400
about the power we have to change our neural&nbsp;
pathways with a pause, and that when you use your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:26.400 --> 00:36:34.000
body as part of that process, there’s something&nbsp;
to it that you can’t just do with your mind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:42.640
It’s definitely the mind-body connection. It’s&nbsp;
critical for me. I do not go a week without yoga.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:42.640 --> 00:36:55.920
I do go to studios now, and I like the slower pace&nbsp;
because it creates that gap between stimulus and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:55.920 --> 00:37:05.200
response. It creates the opportunity for me to be&nbsp;
present in the moment, to make a different choice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:05.200 --> 00:37:13.440
and to slow down. Yoga is definitely a solution&nbsp;
for the mind without involving the mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:13.440 --> 00:37:15.680
without creating the chaos.
37:15&nbsp;

00:37:15.680 --> 00:37:24.320
Jacob: Yoga is so satisfying on every single&nbsp;
